  "prediction": "**Summary of USAID Assistance to the Palestinians in the West Bank and Gaza**\n\nThe United States Agency for International Development (USAID) has provided over $5 billion in bilateral assistance to the Palestinians in the West Bank and Gaza since 1993. This assistance is primarily funded through the Economic Support Fund (ESF) and aims to build a more democratic, stable, and secure Palestinian society.\n\n**Assistance Breakdown**\n\nFor fiscal years 2015 and 2016, USAID obligated approximately $544.1 million and expended about $350.6 million of ESF assistance to the West Bank and Gaza. The assistance was allocated across three development objectives: Economic Growth and Infrastructure (60%), Investing in the Next Generation (27%), and Governance and Civic Engagement (6%). The largest project under Economic Growth and Infrastructure was the Architecture and Engineering Services project, which aimed to rehabilitate and construct infrastructure in the West Bank and Gaza.\n\n**Debt Relief Payments**\n\nUSAID also made direct payments to Palestinian Authority (PA) creditors totaling $145 million from fiscal year 2015 funds and $140 million from fiscal year 2016 funds. These payments were made to two Israeli fuel companies and a Palestinian bank to cover debts for petroleum purchases and medical referrals to hospitals in East Jerusalem.\n\n**Vetting of PA Creditors**\n\nUSAID conducted required vetting of PA creditors to ensure that assistance would not support entities or individuals associated with terrorism. The agency determined that certain legal requirements, including assessments of the PA Ministry of Finance and Planning, were not applicable to direct payments to PA creditors. However, USAID commissioned external assessments and financial audits to ensure the integrity of the debt relief payment process.\n\n**Audit Findings**\n\nThe Government Accountability Office (GAO) conducted an audit of USAID's assistance to the Palestinians in the West Bank and Gaza and found that the agency had complied with relevant laws and regulations. The audit also found that USAID had taken steps to mitigate risks associated with the debt relief payment process, including confirming the vetting status of PA creditors and conducting external assessments and financial audits.\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nUSAID's assistance to the Palestinians in the West Bank and Gaza has contributed to building a more democratic, stable, and secure Palestinian society. The agency has taken steps to ensure the integrity of the debt relief payment process and has complied with relevant laws and regulations. However, the GAO recommends that USAID continue to monitor and evaluate the effectiveness of its assistance programs and take steps to address any identified risks or vulnerabilities.",
